# What the borg manages, so that a drop is noticed.
#
# The nightly cannot use pass and fail for this. Every run dies -- that is what
# unaided play at these levels looks like -- and a run that hits the clock has
# not failed either, so the job goes red only for a crash or a wedge. Without
# this file best depth could halve overnight and the job would stay green,
# which is exactly the regression the nightly exists to catch.
#
# `scripts/borg-progress -b tests/borg/BASELINE` compares a sweep against these
# and fails if a number has dropped materially. Same shape as
# tests/saves/EXPECTED-FAILURES: a committed statement of what is true now,
# which somebody has to change on purpose.
#
# === Updating it when a number legitimately improves ===
#
# Take it from a nightly, not from your own machine. Run the workflow by hand
# (Actions -> Borg -> Run workflow) and copy the summary lines out of its log.
#
# This is not fussiness. The first version of this file was taken from a local
# sweep, and it cost a night: every number differed from CI's, the job called it
# a regression, and the code was fine. The runs are exact to the turn on one
# machine and are not portable between two -- Warrior seed 13 at the same commit
# played 280,358 turns to character level 13 on Darwin arm64 and 5,252 turns to
# level 1 on Linux x86_64. A baseline has to be generated the same way it will
# be measured, and the nightly is what measures it.
#
# `borg-progress` now records the platform below and refuses to compare across
# platforms, so a repeat of that mistake says so instead of failing the build.
# Running a sweep locally against this file is fine; it will skip the check.
#
# Update the notes saying which run reached each best, and say in the commit
# message why the number moved. That last part is the point of the file: a
# baseline that rises without an explanation is a baseline nobody trusts to
# fall.
#
# Do not update it to make a red night go green. If a number dropped and you do
# not know why, that is the finding.
#
# === What these numbers depend on ===
#
# The class list, the seeds, and the turn budget -- all three are in the
# workflow and all three are part of what is being measured. Changing any of
# them invalidates the file, so change them and re-baseline in the same commit.
#
# And the machine. The runs are exact *on one machine*: the same seed on the
# same build gives the same turn count every time, verified three times over,
# and reproduced later at two optimisation levels. They are not exact across
# machines. So nothing here is noise, but everything here is specific to the
# platform named below.
#
# Any change to the game *data* also reshuffles the random stream and moves
# which seed happens to do well -- which is why the thresholds in borg-progress
# tolerate a couple of levels rather than demanding the figures never move at
# all. Note that data changes scoped to something the run does not touch move
# nothing: these runs play a Human, so the four races finished in 3.110.0 left
# every turn count identical, which is how the platform difference was finally
# separated from a real regression.

